  "title": "Capital floods APAC's AI build-out",
  "lede": "The week was defined by large-scale capital formation for AI infrastructure across Asia-Pacific. Alibaba moved to raise US$10.2 billion for AI and data centre expansion, SK Telecom carved out its AI data centre arm as SK Horizon with US$2.2 billion in backing from KKR and an IMM-Stonebridge consortium, and a NYSE-listed buyer agreed to pay more than A$3 billion for Ausgrid's data centre business. Research houses put the region's trajectory in perspective, projecting APAC data centre asset values approaching US$1 trillion by 2030 on roughly US$280 billion of capex. Australia captured much of the operational news: AEMO forecast national data centre power demand rising to 34TWh by 2035-36, new projects advanced in Queensland and Tasmania, and councils and residents pressed for tighter oversight and community benefit deals. Globally, NVIDIA's US$89 billion data centre quarter and AWS's plan to add two million GPUs underscored how AI demand continues to strain power, capacity and supply chains across the region.",
  "themes": [
    {
      "heading": "Capital raises and corporate deals dominate the week",
      "narrative": "Alibaba plans to raise HK$80 billion (US$10.2 billion) through a share placement to fund AI and data centre expansion. SK Telecom carved out its AI data centre operations into SK Horizon, backed by KRW 3.08 trillion in equity from KKR and an IMM Investment-Stonebridge consortium. In Australia, a NYSE-listed digital infrastructure company agreed to acquire Ausgrid's data centre arm for more than A$3 billion, while Blue Owl led a US$3.4 billion debt package for Iren to buy NVIDIA chips. Reported mega-deals extended to compute leasing, with Anthropic said to have struck a US$45 billion agreement with Nscale for more than 400MW of capacity.",
      "story_ids": [
        "2026-08-26-4746ae",
        "2026-08-27-4c1275",
        "2026-08-28-82a841",
        "2026-08-27-719538",
        "2026-08-29-c17897",
        "2026-08-27-10534b"
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "APAC data centre values near US$1 trillion",
      "narrative": "Multiple studies this week projected Asia-Pacific data centre asset values reaching between US$950 billion and US$1 trillion by 2030, underpinned by around US$280 billion in construction spending. Malaysia illustrated the trend at the national level, with MIDA reporting that data centre and cloud projects accounted for nearly half of RM218.5 billion in approved investments in the first half of 2026. Gamuda added RM3.57 billion in contracts to build two hyperscale facilities in Port Dickson.",
      "story_ids": [
        "2026-08-26-e6e710",
        "2026-08-25-d1869b",
        "2026-08-26-997310",
        "2026-08-28-52487d",
        "2026-08-28-50e73f"
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Australia's power crunch shapes data centre policy",
      "narrative": "AEMO's latest outlook projects Australian data centre electricity consumption rising from roughly 5TWh today to 34TWh by 2035-36, a near sevenfold increase confirmed by separate projections. Canberra is preparing new power and water standards for the sector, and Energy Minister Chris Bowen ruled out any carve-outs for Queensland. Industry-commissioned research argued Australia could become an Asia-Pacific AI infrastructure hub and even cut regional emissions by exporting compute, provided grid constraints are resolved.",
      "story_ids": [
        "2026-08-26-0b04cf",
        "2026-08-25-f3bb06",
        "2026-08-25-67fa83",
        "2026-08-28-8bcc87",
        "2026-08-26-b50619",
        "2026-08-26-2df161"
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Approvals advance amid growing local pushback",
      "narrative": "Singapore-based Zerra DC filed plans for a 1.44GW campus in Queensland's Western Downs, and Firmus won council approval for a 288MW AI factory at Bell Bay, Tasmania. NextDC's chief executive said construction timelines should halve to around nine months, while modular specialist DXN won a A$12.2 million containerized data centre contract. The growth is meeting resistance: Whittlesea Council sought greater state oversight of Dover DC's 200MW South Morang project, and developers face mounting pressure for formal community benefit agreements as suburban opposition intensifies.",
      "story_ids": [
        "2026-08-25-e3d1f1",
        "2026-08-26-b5846d",
        "2026-08-27-cdfceb",
        "2026-08-25-5931c7",
        "2026-08-27-32b013",
        "2026-08-26-9f3c92"
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "NVIDIA demand wave strains supply and power",
      "narrative": "NVIDIA reported US$89 billion in data center revenue as AWS moved to deploy two million additional GPUs under an expanded partnership spanning CPUs, networking and robotics. India's Yotta ordered roughly 95,000 GPUs worth US$7.5 billion for its 120MW Delhi facility. NVIDIA pushed its platform roadmap with Vera Rubin's claimed 30-fold throughput gain per megawatt and an expanded NVLink Fusion ecosystem with NVHBM memory, even as it reportedly paused some cloud revenue-sharing deals over antitrust concerns.",
